body:
When trying to write to a field with type Types.TimestampType.withoutZone(), I'm getting the exception
java.time.LocalDateTime cannot be cast to java.time.OffsetDateTime.
This appears to be due to a case match miss in the GenericParquetWriter - for all primitives of type TIMESTAMP_MICROS, the TimestamptzWriter is returned. I believe it should only be returned when shouldAdjustToUTC is true; and the TimestampWriter returned otherwise  (similar to the implementation in GenericParquetReaders).
in GenericParquetWriter:
case TIMESTAMP_MICROS:
    return new TimestamptzWriter(desc);

in GenericParquetReader:
case TIMESTAMP_MICROS:
    TimestampType tsMicrosType = (TimestampType)expected;
    if (tsMicrosType.shouldAdjustToUTC()) {
        return new GenericParquetReaders.TimestamptzReader(desc);
    }

    return new GenericParquetReaders.TimestampReader(desc);

In one of our use cases, we write to many partitions within every batch. As a result, each manifest covers a lot of partitions, which slows down the job planning.
We decided to use RewriteManifests to optimize the layout of the metadata. Our idea was to group files based on their partition values. However, there is a problem. Let's say we have a table with 3 partition columns. If I cluster metadata by all of them, this will produce small manifest files (especially for partitions that don't have all its data yet). I don't want to have too granular manifests but I want all entries for my leaf partitions to be next to each other. If we cluster metadata let's say by the first partition value, then entries for leaf partitions are not next to each other, which slows down the job planning also.
The example above made me think about introducing a sort key in addition to the cluster key so that entries are grouped by that sort key within clusters. The API can look like:
public interface RewriteManifests<C, S>  extends SnapshotUpdate<RewriteManifests<C, S>> {
  RewriteManifests clusterBy(BiFunction<PartitionSpec, DataFile, C> func);
  RewriteManifests sortBy(BiFunction<PartitionSpec, DataFile, S> func, Comparator<S> cmp);
  RewriteManifests rewriteIf(Predicate<ManifestFile> predicate);
}

The problem with the above suggestion is that we will need to keep all the files in memory before writing them to manifests. Metadata rewrites can be expensive and we need a distributed version of it but I am wondering about the overall idea for now. It is clear that such an approach can work only if we compact only a part of the metadata at once.
